-- Version 21 → 22: e-mail becomes the account key.
--
-- `UNIQUE (provider, provider_id)` was the identity, and the schema comment on
-- `users.email` argued at length that an address must never be one — because the
-- same human signing in via both Google and GitHub was two accounts by design.
-- GitHub sign-in is gone and the `AccountMerge` written to clean up after it was
-- retired in 4f05fb0, which left that pair as a more fragile spelling of "the
-- Google account" and foreclosed adding any second way to sign in. See LNL-73 and
-- the rewritten comment in Users.sq.
--
-- Two statements: normalize what is already stored, then make it unique.
--
-- The dual-path story is 1.sqm's and 2.sqm's. MigrationTest migrates every earlier
-- snapshot forward and compares PRAGMA output — indexes included — against a
-- fresh `Schema.create`, so drift between this and Users.sq fails the build
-- rather than the volume.
--
-- ── Why a partial index and not a table rebuild ────────────────────────────
--
-- SQLite cannot `ALTER TABLE ... ADD UNIQUE`. The worked precedent for adding
-- uniqueness is 13.sqm, which rebuilds the table — and 13.sqm itself explains
-- that this was only safe because `attachments` is a leaf that nothing points at.
-- `users` is the exact opposite: sessions, issues (on both `created_by` and
-- `assignee_id`), comments, attachments, roles, subscriptions, ui_settings,
-- email_codes and the oauth_* tables all reference it. Rebuilding it means
-- dropping and recreating every one of those foreign keys correctly, while
-- `migrateSchema` runs with foreign keys OFF (Database.kt) precisely so that sort
-- of thing can go wrong quietly before `verifyForeignKeys` catches it — or
-- doesn't. 3.sqm's warning applies in full.
--
-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... WHERE email IS NOT NULL` needs no ALTER at all, keeps
-- NULLs distinct so unkeyed rows stay legal, and is covered by the existing test.
--
-- ── Normalization first, and what it can collide with ─────────────────────
--
-- Nothing lowercased anywhere before this, and the two write paths disagreed: the
-- OAuth path stored Google's value verbatim, `setEmail` trimmed only. So
-- `Alice@X.com` and `alice@x.com` could be two rows, and the index would refuse
-- to be created over them.
--
-- The deployed volume holds one user, so there is nothing to reconcile and this
-- is the cheapest this change will ever be — that is the reason LNL-73 is being
-- done now rather than later. But "one user" is a fact about today's deployment
-- and not about every volume this migration will ever run on, and a
-- `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX` that fails takes the whole migration — and therefore the
-- boot — down with it. So the third statement below exists as a backstop: where
-- two rows would collide after normalization, the **lowest id keeps the address**
-- and the others are set to NULL.
--
-- That is a real, if tiny, loss: somebody would find their address gone from
-- their profile and their notifications stopped. It is chosen over the
-- alternative deliberately. An un-bootable server is not recoverable by the
-- person it happens to; a null address is visible on the profile screen, fixable
-- by typing it back in, and now — unlike before this ticket — provable. The rows
-- involved were never verified by anything, since nothing could verify anything
-- before LNL-71.
--
-- It should affect nobody. It is here so that if it ever does, the outcome is a
-- support conversation rather than an outage.

-- 1. One spelling. Matches normalizeEmail exactly: trim, then lowercase. Blank
--    becomes NULL for the reason that function gives — "" is not an address, and
--    storing one makes `email IS NOT NULL` true for somebody with nowhere to
--    receive mail.
UPDATE users SET email = NULLIF(LOWER(TRIM(email)), '') WHERE email IS NOT NULL;

-- 2. The backstop described above. Keeps the lowest id per address; clears the
--    rest. A no-op on any volume where no two accounts share an address.
UPDATE users SET email = NULL
WHERE email IS NOT NULL
  AND id > (SELECT MIN(other.id) FROM users other WHERE other.email = users.email);

-- 3. The key itself.
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users(email) WHERE email IS NOT NULL;
